 Qiskit SDK v2.6 release summary
 Technical release summary for Qiskit SDK v2.6, including updates on top new features, breaking changes, and our ongoing efforts to make Qiskit the world's most performant quantum SDK.

 Date
 22 Oct 2026

 Authors
 Qiskit Team

 Topics
 Software Updates 
 Qiskit 

 Share this blog

 Was this blog post helpful?
 Yes No 

 Key takeaways: 

 Qiskit SDK v2.6 is a C API–focused release, bringing the compiler's pass-manager framework, QPY serialization, and custom operations to compiled languages.

 A new C API pass-manager framework lets you write custom compiler passes and lower circuits through multiple type-checked intermediate representations.

 QPY serialization is now in the C API, enabling programs in other languages including C, C++, and Julia to save and exchange circuits compatible with Python's qiskit.qpy .

 You can define custom quantum operations, including hardware-native gates, directly from the C API without Python's object model.

 Qiskit v2.6 makes progress toward enabling use of the C API in environments where Python isn't installed.

 QPY format v18 is now the default and shrinks serialized circuits; minimum Python stays 3.10, source builds need Rust 1.89.

 We're excited to announce the release of Qiskit SDK v2.6! The latest minor release in the v2.x series continues our effort to strengthen the SDK's core infrastructure—making it faster, more extensible, and more capable as a high-performance compiler for quantum computing.

 A central story of recent releases has been Qiskit's evolution beyond Python, exposing its high-performance Rust core through a C API that interoperates with nearly every language and opens the door to the tightly-coupled, quantum-centric supercomputing (QCSC) workflows that HPC environments demand. Qiskit v2.6 is, above all, a C API release: it brings the compiler's pass-manager framework, QPY serialization, and custom operations to the C API, and continues the behind-the-scenes work of making it possible to use the C API without having Python installed.

 These capabilities are more than conveniences for non-Python users. A compiler that lowers a program through multiple intermediate representations, a serialization format that moves circuits between languages and systems, and an execution model that puts composable control in the user's hands—these are the foundational plumbing for what's next.

 That groundwork points toward the QCSC and fault-tolerant workloads to come, even as Qiskit keeps delivering real value for near-term work today. Qiskit is being built to be the performant software stack users reach for across that entire continuum—not only for near-term work, where it already delivers real value, but for the error-corrected pipelines as well.

 The TL;DR

 Qiskit SDK v2.6 is a release centered on the C API. Previous releases gave C API users the ability to build circuits and run them through Qiskit's transpiler; v2.6 gives them far more control—a customizable, extensible pass-manager framework (including lowering through multiple IRs), QPY serialization, and the ability to define their own operations—all without Python. Highlights include:

 A new pass-manager framework in the C API. You can now write and execute compiler passes directly from C, and lower a program through multiple intermediate representations (IRs) with type-checked, cross-language pipelines.

 QPY serialization in the C API. Qiskit's portable binary circuit format can now be written and read from C, operating on native circuits without a Python round-trip, and remaining compatible with Python-produced QPY.

 Custom operations defined from C. You can now fully define quantum operations directly from the C API and add them to any circuit.

 Continued progress toward making Python optional. Behind-the-scenes work moves the standalone C library closer to running without Python being installed—a stepping stone toward, for example, letting Julia-on-Windows users run Qiskit .

 A new QPY format version (v18). 2.6 bumps the QPY format with several improvements (see Upgrade considerations).

 Top new features and improvements

 New pass-manager framework in the C API

 Qiskit v2.6 introduces a generic pass-manager framework to the C API. C API users could already run Qiskit's preset transpilation pipeline; now they can build their own—writing individual compiler passes and composing them into custom pass managers, all from compiled languages.

 The new QkPassManager manages execution. It can be populated with QkPass objects via qk_passmanager_push_pass and run with qk_passmanager_run_simple ; the pass manager and its passes report errors through QkCompilationError . The functionality of a pass is defined through a vtable ( QkPassVtable ). Passes declare their input and output IR with a QkIrHandle , which can describe either a dynamically defined C IR or a built-in Qiskit IR (today, QkCircuit and QkDag ). The pass manager uses these handles to verify the pipeline—enabling type-checked compiler pipelines .

 Crucially, this is the piece that lets a program be lowered through multiple IRs : because each pass declares the IR type it consumes and produces, the pass manager can validate that a multi-stage lowering pipeline is well-formed, and extensions are free to define their own IR types in addition to Qiskit's built-ins.

 This continues the multi-representation compiler framework introduced in v2.5, bringing that layered, progressively-lowering compiler model to the C API. It's a foundational step toward a compiler that can take a program from a high-level description down to hardware-specific operations across multiple optimization levels.

 The example below defines a custom pass—here, one that removes identity-equivalent gates by calling an existing Qiskit routine—registers it in a vtable, and runs it on a circuit through a QkPassManager :

 #include <qiskit.h> 

 /// A struct to keep the configuration of the pass. 
 typedef struct { 
 QkTarget * target; 
 } RemoveIdentity; 

 /// The pass execution (or `run` function). 
 QkCircuit * run_remove_identity (RemoveIdentity * self , QkCircuit * ir , QkPassContext * context , 
 QkCompilationError ** error) { 
 // You can implement the custom logic in this function. Here, 
 // we're calling a predefined Qiskit function to remove identity gates. 
 qk_transpiler_pass_standalone_remove_identity_equivalent(ir , self -> target , 1.0 ) ; 
 return ir; 
 } 

 /// The vtable defining the pass. 
 static const QkVtableEntry remove_identity_slots [] = { 
 {.slot = QkPassSlot_RunOwned , .ptr = run_remove_identity} , 
 {.slot = - 1 , .ptr = NULL } , 
 }; 

 int main ( void ) { 
 // A handle to the IR the pass is defined on. This can also be defined 
 // as a custom IR; see `qk_ir_handle_new`. 
 QkIrHandle * ir = qk_ir_handle_builtin(QkIrBuiltin_Circuit) ; 
 // An instance of a vtable which also defines the name and in/out IRs. 
 QkPassVtable * vtable = qk_pass_vtable_new( "remove_identity" , ir , ir , remove_identity_slots) ; 

 // Set up the pass... 
 QkTarget * target = qk_target_new( 10 ) ; 
 RemoveIdentity this = {target}; 
 QkPass * pass = qk_pass_new(( void * ) & this , vtable) ; 

 // ... and add it to the pass manager 
 QkPassManager * pm = qk_passmanager_new() ; 
 qk_passmanager_push_pass(pm , pass) ; 

 // Now we can execute it on a circuit. 
 QkCircuit * circuit = qk_circuit_new( 10 , 0 ) ; 
 uint32_t q0[ 1 ] = { 0 }; 
 double almost_zero[ 1 ] = { 1 e- 20 }; 
 double nonzero[ 1 ] = { 1.23 }; 
 qk_circuit_gate(circuit , QkGate_H , q0 , NULL ) ; 
 qk_circuit_gate(circuit , QkGate_RX , q0 , almost_zero) ; 
 qk_circuit_gate(circuit , QkGate_RZ , q0 , nonzero) ; 
 qk_circuit_gate(circuit , QkGate_H , q0 , NULL ) ; 

 // note: The pass manager takes ownership of the input IR, which no longer 
 // needs to be freed -- only the output IR must be freed. 
 QkCircuit * out = (QkCircuit * ) qk_passmanager_run_simple(pm , ( void * )circuit , ir , ir , NULL ) ; 

 qk_circuit_free(out) ; 
 qk_target_free(target) ; 
 qk_passmanager_free(pm) ; 
 qk_pass_vtable_free(vtable) ; 
 qk_ir_handle_free(ir) ; 

 return 0 ; 
 } 

 Copy to clipboard 

 The pass declares the same built-in IR ( QkIrBuiltin_Circuit ) for both input and output; a lowering pass would instead declare different input and output IR handles, and the pass manager would type-check the transition.

 QPY serialization in the C API

 QPY—Qiskit's portable, cross-platform binary format for storing quantum circuits—is now accessible from the C API. Multiple QkCircuit objects can be serialized into a single QPY stream, either to an in-memory buffer ( qk_qpy_dump_buffer ) or to a file ( qk_qpy_dump_file ), with *_with_version variants to pin the QPY version used. Circuits can be loaded back with qk_qpy_load_file / qk_qpy_load_buffer . Qiskit v2.6 also introduces QPY format v18, now the default, which reduces the size of serialized circuits.

 These functions operate on native circuits without interacting with Python , and the data they produce is compatible with the Python qiskit.qpy interface—so a circuit serialized from a C program (or any language calling the C API) can be loaded in Python, and vice versa. (If you're using the C API from inside a Python extension, you should still use the Python qpy.dump / qpy.load functions; the C functions return an error if Python is required to create or load the payload.)

 For compiled-language and HPC users, this unlocks two concrete workflows:

 Persistence across runs. QPY was designed from the start to save circuits to disk and reload them in a later execution, but before Qiskit v2.6, that was only possible from Python. You can now build and compile a circuit in C—or in another compiled language through its C FFI, such as C++, Julia, or Fortran—cache it to disk, and reload it in a subsequent run without ever standing up a Python interpreter.

 Job submission. QPY is also the format Qiskit uses to submit circuits
